      "notes": "Verdict is well-constructed. The too-uncertain direction is genuinely supported by competing plausible channels (better accountability vs. disclosure risk) with no quantified evidence to resolve the question. Tier assignments are appropriate: E3 (Andrew Tyrie/Constitution Unit assessment) is borderline \u2014 an expert opinion stated as measurable fact \u2014 but it is quoted with attribution and the claim hedges slightly ('has been assessed as'), making it acceptable rather than blocking. E14 (Lord Beamish) is advocacy from a directly interested party but is used only to illustrate a corroborating governance concern, not to drive magnitude. The rationale correctly notes evidence addresses governance legitimacy rather than security-outcome effect sizes. Crux parameter and estimate range are substantive, not lazy hedges. No party identity or motive attribution detected. No lever leakage \u2014 the verdict explicitly notes the policy primarily registers on O9 and correctly reasons through the O5 channel only. False balance check passes: both channels are named and grounded in evidence (E3/E14 for accountability improvement; E4 for disclosure risk)."
